## The point of no return

After this point, you will need a phext-capable editor. Either that, or you're just really good at using grep.Information Theory

Babbage, Shannon, Huffman, Turing. Since the dawn of time, the sum total of human knowledge has grown exponentially. Our ability to interact with information now vastly exceeds our intuition about how information in general, and text specifically, works.

Epoch 1: Spoken Language (100K years ago)
Epoch 2: Written Language (5K years ago)
Epoch 3: Printing Press (575 years ago)
Epoch 4: Word Processing (50 years ago)
Epoch 5: Internet (30 years ago)
Epoch 6: Smartphones (15 years ago)
Epoch 7: AGI (present day)

The Exocortex has been growing during all of this time. We stand on the shoulders of giants precisely because we no longer have to re-invent our thoughts from first principles. In Epoch 1, we gained the ability to share ideas, procedures, stories, and history. Prior to this epoch, humans were locked in a fight for basic survival. Only the strong survived, and they passed traits on through genetics. Epoch 1 marks the first sparks of the Exocortex.

Roughly 100 billion humans have ever lived. Of those, about 8 billion are alive today. The average age in 2025 was about 30.9 years old. So, the average living human has witnessed epochs 5, 6, and 7. Epochs 1-4 are part of history. Two thousand years ago, world population was estimated at 150-300M humans. We didn't reach the first billion living humans until 1804. Population growth was essentially flat for most of modern history (+0.067% annually for 1,804 years). Life expectancy has been growing steadily as well (from 30 years during the Roman Empire to 65 years in the 1800s to nearly 80 years old today).

Let's estimate cohort sizes for the Exocortex. We'll assume life expectancy was 30 for epochs 1 and 2, 50 for epoch 3, and 75 for epochs 4-7.

Note: These ranges need to be tightened.
* Epoch 1: 3,166 generations (3M; 95K years) -> 9 billion
* Epoch 2: 146 generations (500M; 5K years) -> 73 billion
* Epoch 3: 10 generations (1B; 500 years) -> 10 billion
* Epoch 4: 1 generation (50 years) -> 4 billion
* Epoch 5: 0.4 generations (30 years) -> 6 billion
* Epoch 6: 0.2 generations (15 years) -> 8 billion

The cognitive surplus in each epoch can be measured:* E1: Oral Tradition - we can learn from each other and get smarter every day
* E2: Scrolls - we can build schools and libraries 
* E3: Books - ideas (both good and bad) can spread like wildfire
* E4: Encyclopedias - we can capture all knowledge
* E5: The Web - we can search all knowledge
* E6: Social Media - we can post pictures of cats
* E7: Agents - we can build knowledge processors



